  "PROGRAM_ENG": {
   "null_rate": 0.57,
   "unique": 33,
   "top": {
    "\r\n                                        \r\n                                        &lt;p style=&quot;text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family: &#39;맑은 고딕&#39;; color: black; font-size: 11pt;&quot;&gt;Everyone quietly agrees that the year must end with Beethoven’s Ninth, and 2027 is no exception. But is that enough on its own? This year something new comes first. Principal timpanist Omar El-Abidin plays Thierry Escaich’s Timpani Concerto, co-commissioned with the Orchestre Philharmonique de Radio France and the NFM Wrocław Philharmonic. Then comes the ‘Choral’ Symphony. Why must December belong to it? Because nothing else will do, and it will comfort everyone who has lived through 2027.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\r\n                                    \r\n                                    ": 3,
    "&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: &#39;맑은 고딕&#39;;&quot;&gt;Life is full of suffering, Mahler realised early on. Yet for him suffering was neither a tragedy nor a final destination, and he gave everything to turning it into symphonies. Now past its halfway point, the orchestra’s Mahler cycle opens its 2027 chapter with the Fifth. An ominous, majestic trumpet fanfare leads into a funeral march that trudges towards fate. A stormy second movement follows, then a third close to a horn concerto, the Adagietto for strings and harp alone, and a vast festive finale. The journey leads from pain to triumph, but never along a tidy path. Surprises great and small wait at every turn.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;": 3,
    "\r\n                                        &lt;p style=&quot;text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; font-family: &#39;맑은 고딕&#39;;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt;&quot;&gt;Bleak winds outside, warmth within: that is Sibelius. Finnish conductor Jukka-Pekka Saraste has long championed his compatriot, and here pairs two works born of Maeterlinck’s &lt;/span&gt;&lt;i&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt;&quot;&gt;Pelléas et Mélisande&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt;&quot;&gt;. After Sibelius’s suite, Daniel Lozakovich, who played Saint-Saëns’s Third Concerto here in 2023, returns for Sibelius’s Violin Concerto. Schönberg’s vast &lt;/span&gt;&lt;i&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt;&quot;&gt;Pelleas und Melisande&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt;&quot;&gt;, written before his twelve-tone years, takes tonality to its limits.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\r\n                                    ": 2,
    "\r\n                                        &lt;p style=&quot;text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family: &#39;맑은 고딕&#39;; font-size: 11pt;&quot;&gt;A vast symphony that never flaunts its ambition. After the Fifth in March, the orchestra’s second Mahler of 2027 is the Third, whose sheer forces and length demand careful thought before a note is played. The first of its six movements alone runs over half an hour. A minuet and a scherzo follow. Mezzo-soprano Tone Kummervold sings in the fourth movement, and women’s chorus and boys’ choir join in the fifth. The long closing Adagio feels like letting go of everything at last. Jaap van Zweden and the orchestra trace one man’s fierce will to embrace nature, humanity and boundless love in music.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\r\n                                    ": 2,
    "&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family: &#39;맑은 고딕&#39;; font-size: 11pt;&quot;&gt;Augustin Hadelich, a trusted friend of the orchestra, returns with a work that makes this visit especially welcome. Since 2026 he has played Beethoven&#39;s Violin Concerto with the Berlin Philharmonic and the Staatskapelle Dresden, and knows it intimately. The piece is hard to play, he says, but so fine that nothing needs to be added. Le Sacre du printemps brings a stark contrast. Van Zweden, who recorded it with the New York Philharmonic, leads a score demanding razor-sharp rhythm and total precision.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;": 2,
    "&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family: &#39;맑은 고딕&#39;; font-size: 11pt;&quot;&gt;No concert planner would turn down this programme. Rachmaninoff’s Second Piano Concerto and Tchaikovsky’s Fifth Symphony are among Korea’s best-loved works, and together they all but guarantee a full house. Yet familiar music puts performers under pressure, because listeners know every note. Yekwon Sunwoo has built much of his career on Rachmaninoff and says he has come to feel close to the composer. Tchaikovsky’s journey from heavy sorrow to final joy then delivers the emotion audiences come for. It has never let them down.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;": 2,
    "&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: &#39;맑은 고딕&#39;;&quot;&gt;Earl Lee returns after six years with a programme uniting orchestra and chorus. Samy Moussa&#39;s shimmering Elysium leads to Mendelssohn&#39;s Violin Concerto with James Ehnes. With the Prague Philharmonic Choir, Brahms&#39;s Schicksalslied sings of heaven and the fate of those who cannot reach it, and Ravel&#39;s Daphnis et Chloé Suite No. 2 floats distant voices over a full orchestra.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;": 2,
    "&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family: &#39;맑은 고딕&#39;;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: &#39;맑은 고딕&#39;;&quot;&gt;New stars are rare on the podium, but Roderick Cox shines especially bright. After first meeting the orchestra in Shostakovich’s Tenth in 2026, he makes his subscription series debut. Barber’s urbane Second Essay and Shostakovich’s First Cello Concerto, with Alban Gerhardt, show two faces of the twentieth century. Strauss’s &lt;/span&gt;&lt;i&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: &#39;맑은 고딕&#39;;&quot;&gt;Ein Heldenleben&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: &#39;맑은 고딕&#39;;&quot;&gt; (1898), a last bastion of nineteenth-century music, anticipates the century to come while holding fast to the classical past.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;": 2,
    "\r\n                                        &lt;p style=&quot;text-align: justify; line-height: 1.5;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family: &#39;맑은 고딕&#39;; font-size: 11pt;&quot;&gt;Who was Beethoven, really? Here is one more answer. The series ends with his rarely heard chamber music with winds: the six-movement Serenade Op. 25, recalling Haydn and Mozart; the Trio WoO 37, written at fifteen; and the Quintet Op. 16, where the piano replaces the flute of a standard wind quintet. The day after her Strauss concerto, oboist Cristina Gómez Godoy joins orchestra musicians to reveal an entirely different Beethoven.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\r\n                                    ": 1,
    "&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 1.5; text-align: justify;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family: &#39;맑은 고딕&#39;; font-size: 11pt;&quot;&gt;Western music has long aimed to balance four voices: bass, tenor, alto and soprano. That is why the string quartet became central to chamber music. A string trio has one voice too few and a quintet one too many, and the viola steps forward to resolve the imbalance. Violist Tabea Zimmermann leads Beethoven’s early String Trio in G major and his String Quintet in C major. Following Mozart’s model with two violas, it is effectively his only work in the genre.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;": 1
   },
   "type": "code"
  },
